From: PowerVLC <powervlc@localhost>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 18:25:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ppc/hevc: AltiVec SAO, inverse transform and motion compensation

Measured on a 1.42 GHz 7447A (Mac mini G4, Leopard), single-threaded, with an
isolated ffmpeg decode bench. Decoded output is bit-identical to the C
reference on every sample, checked with a hash of all decoded planes:

                          reference   +SAO+IDCT   +MC   +chroma/w   +whole-pel
   854x480  10-bit HEVC    16.8        22.2       24.2    24.9        26.9 fps (+60 %)
  1080x1920 10-bit HEVC     3.17        4.18       4.79    5.26           -- fps
  1080x1920  8-bit HEVC     3.50        4.91       5.87    6.38           -- fps

A profile of a 10-bit 1080x1920 decode attributed ~39 % of the time to motion
compensation, ~20 % to SAO and ~19 % to the inverse transform. None of the
three had a PowerPC path: ff_hevc_dsp_init_ppc only installed idct_4x4, which
does not even show up in the profile.

SAO: both filters are a small table lookup plus an add and a clip per pixel,
which is one vec_perm plus a saturating add on sixteen (8-bit) or eight
(10-bit) pixels at a time.

IDCT: eight neighbouring columns are transformed in parallel so every lane
wants the same constant and no horizontal reduction is needed; the second pass
runs on the transposed block, as in the x86 SSE2 version. col_limit is ignored,
also as in the x86 version -- it only promises that some coefficients are zero.

MC: the 8-tap luma and 4-tap chroma FIRs, eight output samples at a time.
Vertically the lanes are neighbouring columns so the taps are plain strided
loads; horizontally they are the same vector shifted one sample at a time via
vec_sld. Storing is the awkward part, because the uni and bi variants write
pixels into the frame at whatever alignment the prediction unit sits at and
AltiVec has no unaligned store (in 8-bit, eight pixels are only half a vector).
It uses the same idiom as this fork's H.264 qpel8 kernels: rotate with
vec_lvsr, then write with element stores, which touch exactly the bytes asked
for and never a neighbour. The element width is chosen once per call from the
alignment of the destination and its stride -- a word store needs a 4-byte
aligned address and 8-bit chroma only guarantees 2. Covered: h, v and hv for
put, uni, bi and uni_w, luma and chroma; bi_w and the whole-pel copies stay in
C (the latter are already memcpy).

Big-endian only: the code is written for the classic AltiVec load/permute
idioms, and every target of this fork is big-endian.
